## 2. Fundamental Terms Used in NLP

Understanding the basic terminology of NLP is important because these concepts describe how language data is collected, organized, and prepared for machine learning.

The following terms can be explained using an example dataset containing movie reviews.

| Term | Definition | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Corpus | A large collection of text documents used for linguistic analysis or NLP research. | A collection of 1,000 movie reviews. |
| Document | A single unit of text within a corpus. | One movie review. |
| Token | A unit of text produced by splitting text into smaller parts, usually words or punctuation marks. | "The movie was excellent" contains four word tokens. |
| Vocabulary | The collection of unique tokens found in a corpus. | The, movie, was, excellent, boring. |
| Feature | A measurable or numerical property extracted from text and used by a machine learning model. | The number of times the word "excellent" appears. |
| Label | The target category assigned to a document during supervised learning. | Positive or negative. |

### Example Dataset

Suppose we have the following movie reviews:

| Document | Review | Label |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | The movie was excellent | Positive |
| D2 | The movie was boring | Negative |
| D3 | Excellent acting and story | Positive |

From this dataset:

- **Corpus:** All three movie reviews together.
- **Documents:** D1, D2, and D3.
- **Tokens:** Words such as "movie", "excellent", and "boring".
- **Vocabulary:** The unique words appearing across the three reviews.
- **Features:** Word counts, such as how many times "excellent" appears in each review.
- **Labels:** Positive and negative, which indicate the sentiment of each review.

These concepts help NLP researchers organize text and convert it into a format suitable for computational analysis.

