## 6. Challenges of NLP for Low-Resource Languages Such as Somali

Low-resource languages are languages that have limited digital text collections, linguistic tools, annotated datasets, and computational resources available for developing NLP systems.

Somali is an important example of a language that faces several resource-related challenges in computational linguistics.

Although Somali is widely spoken in the Horn of Africa, developing accurate NLP applications for it remains difficult.

The following are four major challenges.

### 6.1 Limited Availability of Datasets

One major challenge is the shortage of large, high-quality Somali text datasets.

Many advanced NLP models are trained using large collections of text. However, Somali has fewer publicly available and carefully annotated datasets compared with languages such as English.

**Effect on NLP Systems:**

- Machine learning models have fewer examples from which to learn.
- Models may struggle to recognize Somali expressions accurately.
- Training and evaluating NLP systems becomes more difficult.
- The performance of applications such as translation and sentiment analysis may be limited.

For example, a sentiment analysis model trained using a very small Somali dataset may fail to recognize different expressions of happiness, dissatisfaction, or sarcasm.

Badel et al. (2023) highlighted the shortage of dedicated Somali information retrieval resources and developed a corpus to address this problem.

### 6.2 Spelling Variation and Inconsistent Writing

Another challenge is variation in how Somali speakers write words, particularly in informal digital communication.

Social media users may use different spellings, abbreviations, or informal expressions when writing the same word or communicating the same meaning.

**Effect on NLP Systems:**

- A computer may interpret different spellings as separate words.
- Search systems may fail to retrieve relevant documents.
- Classification models may not recognize familiar words written in unfamiliar ways.
- The vocabulary becomes unnecessarily large.

For example, if users write the same expression using different spelling conventions, a system that has only learned one form may not recognize the alternatives.

This makes text normalization and the development of consistent linguistic resources important.

### 6.3 Morphological Complexity and Different Word Forms

Somali has rich morphology, meaning that words can take different forms through grammatical changes and the addition of affixes.

A single root word may produce several related forms.

**Effect on NLP Systems:**

- A model may treat related word forms as unrelated vocabulary items.
- Search systems may miss documents containing different forms of a query word.
- Text classification becomes more difficult when the model has not encountered certain forms during training.
- Stemming and lemmatization become more challenging.

For example, lemmatization can help identify the underlying root of different word forms, allowing an NLP system to recognize their relationship.

Mohamed and Mohamed (2023) investigated a lexicon-based and rule-based lemmatization approach specifically for Somali.

### 6.4 Code-Switching and Mixed-Language Text

Code-switching occurs when speakers use two or more languages within the same conversation or text.

For example, a Somali social media user might combine Somali and English in one sentence.

**Effect on NLP Systems:**

- The model may struggle to identify which language individual words belong to.
- Language-specific grammar and vocabulary may become mixed.
- Translation systems may produce inaccurate results.
- Sentiment analysis may fail to understand the intended meaning.

This challenge is particularly important when developing NLP applications for social media, where informal and multilingual communication is common.

### Summary of Somali NLP Challenges

| Challenge | Description | Effect on NLP |
|---|---|---|
| Limited datasets | Few large, annotated Somali text collections | Reduces training and evaluation opportunities |
| Spelling variation | Inconsistent spelling and informal writing | Makes word recognition and search difficult |
| Morphological complexity | Words have different grammatical forms | Creates difficulties in word analysis and matching |
| Code-switching | Somali mixed with other languages | Makes language identification and interpretation harder |

**Conclusion:** Developing effective Somali NLP systems requires more high-quality datasets, better language-processing tools, consistent text resources, and research that considers the linguistic characteristics of Somali.

