## Conclusion

Natural Language Processing is an important area of Artificial Intelligence that enables computers to work with human language.

This assignment has examined the fundamental concepts of NLP, including corpora, documents, tokens, vocabulary, features, and labels. It has also discussed four common NLP tasks and explained the differences between supervised and unsupervised learning.

Furthermore, it has demonstrated why text must be converted into numerical representations before most machine learning algorithms can process it.

An important finding from this research is that low-resource languages such as Somali face difficulties related to limited datasets, spelling variation, morphological complexity, and code-switching.

The Somali sentiment analysis case study demonstrates that useful NLP applications can be developed even when language resources are limited.

Overall, improving NLP for Somali and other African languages requires continued research, better datasets, linguistic resources, and the development of language-specific computational tools.

